uct a statement that also must be true:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}w≡{{field-2}}z\\,(\\text{mod}{{field-3}})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11d5fb97-c633-54cb-abf0-947c2b0b0a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413569 #edb/ref 1074 "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's multiply our congruence by $2$ and apply the second property.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\, & ⟹\\,2\\cdot w≡2\\cdot z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 8) \\\\ w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\, & ⟹\\,2w≡2z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413570 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$120\\cdot 140\\text{ mod }9=$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33e605f0-847a-5675-b130-592bda56586a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413570 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & ≡13\\cdot 9+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡13\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 140 & ≡15\\cdot 9+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡15\\cdot 0+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\cdot 140 & ≡3\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡15\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡9+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n120\\cdot 140\\text{ mod }9=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413571 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413571 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413571 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413571 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $7x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40d1d6db-2cfa-5253-ab0a-58b0f4105c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413571 #edb/ref 1074 "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $7,$ and $3\\cdot 7=21$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 3\\cdot 7x & ≡3\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 21x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+20x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+0x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413572 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413572 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413572 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${12}^{17}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42a09307-b06f-57dd-a795-e7f9c01844a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413572 #edb/ref 1074 "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & ≡10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{4} & ≡{2}^{4}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {12}^{8} & ≡{6}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 10+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${12}^{17}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{12}^{17}={({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{17} & ≡{({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(6)^{2}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡72\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10=2\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${12}^{17}$ is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413573 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$113\\cdot 119\\text{ mod }10=$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a7e58ae-7402-5947-902d-187649afe03f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413573 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113 & ≡11\\cdot 10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 119 & ≡11\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113\\cdot 119 & ≡3\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n113\\cdot 119\\text{ mod }10=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413574 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413574 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413574 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413574 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$83\\cdot 96\\text{ mod }10=$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f365d70-97c4-5d2a-be4d-f7de85215156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413574 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{a