ref 1069 "q-213057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413583 #edb/ref 1071 "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${15}^{4}≡$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d32d1e8e-c6cb-5e8e-ad44-72ef38726d73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413583 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}=(3\\cdot 5)^{4}={3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 7+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{15}^{4} & ≡{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 7+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}≡1\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413584 #edb/ref 1071 "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${20}^{4}≡$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8e0aa45-c7f0-5602-aeab-6e88c994c623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413584 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}=(4\\cdot 5)^{4}={4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 6+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{20}^{4} & ≡{4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}≡4\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413585 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$94\\cdot 74\\text{ mod }8=$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e40b6e95-46c9-501b-9cf3-a3ca43d4de46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413585 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}94 & ≡11\\cdot 8+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 74 & ≡9\\cdot 8+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}94\\cdot 74 & ≡6\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n94\\cdot 74\\text{ mod }8=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $u≡v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ construct a statement that also must be true:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}u≡{{field-2}}v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eca33d65-a659-5a03-8cda-7de1fade1db7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413586 #edb/ref 1074 "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nSince the modulus is unchanged in both statements, let's multiply by $5$ and apply the first property.\n\n$$\nu≡v\\,⟹\\,5u≡5v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
